// NOTE: every filter combination (brand, category, size, color, in_stock,
// price, gender, fabric, search, sort) goes through the SAME endpoint
// (AllProductsApi) as comma-separated params, e.g.:
//   page=1&limit=16&search=tshirts&brand_id=1,2,3&category_id=132
//   &sort=newest&size=XL&in_stock=true&color=Black&min_price=20&max_price=40
//   &gender=UNISEX&fabric=COTTON
// If your backend doesn't yet accept category_id / size / color / in_stock /
// min_price / max_price / gender / fabric on that endpoint, those params
// just need to be added server-side.
//
// CATEGORY SELECTION — category_id supports checking sub-categories under
// MULTIPLE different top-level (grand) categories at the same time, e.g.
// Apparel & Uniforms > T-Shirt AND Business Cards > Standard checked
// together sends category_id=<tshirt_id>,<standard_id>. See `activeParents`
// (cross-category) and `buildFilterParams` below.
//
// IMPORTANT — grand-category click behavior:
// Clicking a GRAND (top-level) category's NAME never applies a whole-category
// filter by itself when that category has sub-categories. It only opens the
// header's hover dropdown / toggles the sidebar's expand row so the person can
// see and check specific sub-categories. Actual filtering for such categories
// happens ONLY via the sub-category checkboxes (`toggleSubCategory`), which
// write into `activeParents`. The only case a grand-category click filters
// directly is when that category has NO sub-categories at all (a leaf), since
// there's no finer-grained option to fall back to.
//
// INDUSTRY SELECTION — mirrors the Category tree, but ends one level deeper:
// Industry (grand) -> Use Case (mid) -> real, checkable categories
// (`parent_categories`, same shape/ids as the plain Category tree). Checking
// one of these categories filters products via the SAME `category_id` param
// as the Category tree — merged with `activeParents` — NOT via a separate
// `use_case_id` param. Clicking a Use Case itself is a "select all"
// shortcut: it checks/unchecks every real category listed under it in one
// go (`toggleUseCaseCategories`), but is still expressed purely as those
// categories' ids in `category_id` — a Use Case's own id is never sent to
// the API. See `activeIndustryCategories`, `toggleIndustryCategory`, and
// `toggleUseCaseCategories` below.
//
// This view now exposes filtering in TWO places that both read/write the
// SAME shared state, so they never fall out of sync:
//   1. The sticky HEADER nav (All / Brands mega-menu / Category tabs with a
//      hover sub-category dropdown) — for fast, top-of-page navigation.
//   2. The SIDEBAR (Category tree, Brands, Price, Size, Color, Gender,
//      Fabric, Availability) — for fine-grained, multi-facet filtering.
//
// SEARCH-CLEARING RULE — applying ANY filter or clicking any category
// (header tabs, header sub-nav pills, brand mega-menu, sidebar category
// tree, sidebar industry tree, sizes, colors, genders, fabrics, in-stock,
// price, or "Clear all") drops the active `search` term from both the URL
// and the header search input via `clearActiveSearch` / the `clearSearch`
// flag on `syncQueryString`, so a stale search term never gets silently
// combined with a newly-applied filter in the API call. Changing SORT is
// deliberately exempt — sorting existing search results shouldn't wipe them.
